  What was the result of a "Div-only" structure on code maintainability?
A)It was easier to debug
B)It was difficult because every section looked identical in the code   //answers
C)It made the file size smaller
D)It prevented CSS from working


✅ Correct Answer: B. It was difficult because every section looked identical in the code.

Easy English:

In HTML4, developers used only <div> tags for every part of the webpage.

Example:

<div>Header</div>
<div>Menu</div>
<div>Content</div>
<div>Footer</div>

All sections use the same <div> tag, so:

❌ It is hard to know which section is which.
❌ The code is difficult to read.
❌ The code is difficult to maintain (update or fix).
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠

Many <div> = Many Problems

Div-only → Confusing code → Hard to maintain
Semantic tags (<header>, <nav>, <main>, <footer>) → Clear code → Easy to maintain
One-Line Memory:

Only <div> = All sections look the same = Hard to maintain.

✅ Answer: B. It was difficult because every section looked identical in the code.




2.Does a <footer> always have to be at the very bottom of the entire web page?
A)Yes
B)No, it can be the footer of an <article> or <section>  //answers
C)Only if it contains copyright
D)Only in mobile view


Correct Answer: B. No, it can be the footer of an <article> or <section>

Easy English Explanation:

The <footer> tag does not always mean the bottom of the whole webpage.

It can be used:

✅ At the bottom of the entire page.
✅ At the bottom of an <article>.
✅ At the bottom of a <section>.
Example:
<article>
  <h2>HTML5</h2>
  <p>Learning HTML5 is fun.</p>

  <footer>
    Written by Gowtham
  </footer>
</article>

Here, the <footer> belongs to the article, not the whole webpage.

Easy Trick to Remember 🧠

Footer = End of a section, not always end of the page.

Page ends → Page footer
Article ends → Article footer
Section ends → Section footer
One-Line Memory:

<footer> = End of any content (page, article, or section).

✅ Answer: B. No, it can be the footer of an <article> or <section>.


  3.What is the main structural difference between <div> and <section>?
A)<div> is block, <section> is inline
B)<div> has no meaning, <section> implies a thematic groupin   ///answers
C)<div> is newer
D)<div> only works in HTML4


  ✅ Correct Answer: B. <div> has no meaning, <section> implies a thematic grouping.

Easy English Explanation
<div>
A generic container.
It has no meaning.
Used mainly for CSS and JavaScript.

Example:

<div class="box">
  Welcome
</div>
<section>
A semantic tag.
It has meaning.
Used to group related content (one topic).

Example:

<section>
  <h2>About Us</h2>
  <p>We build websites.</p>
</section>
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
<div> = Box 📦 (No meaning)
<section> = Chapter 📖 (One topic, has meaning)
One-Line Memory:

<div> = Just a container.
<section> = A container with a topic.

✅ Answer: B. <div> has no meaning, <section> implies a thematic grouping.


