5.Which SEO drawback did HTML4 have regarding content prioritization?
A)Search engines couldn't easily tell which content was the primary article  // answer
B)Search engines couldn't read DIVs
C)HTML4 was too slow
D)Text was not indexable


✅ Correct Answer: A. Search engines couldn't easily tell which content was the primary article.

Easy English Explanation

In HTML4, developers mostly used <div> tags.

Example:

<div>Header</div>
<div>Menu</div>
<div>Article</div>
<div>Footer</div>

All sections looked the same, so search engines (like Google) could not easily understand which part was the main content.

In HTML5, semantic tags make this clear:

<header>...</header>
<nav>...</nav>
<main>...</main>
<article>...</article>
<footer>...</footer>

Now search engines can easily identify the main article, which helps with SEO (Search Engine Optimization).

Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
HTML4 → Only <div> → Google gets confused 😕
HTML5 → <main> + <article> → Google understands 👍
One-Line Memory:

HTML4 = No clear main content → Poor SEO.

✅ Answer: A. Search engines couldn't easily tell which content was the primary article.


6.Which element serves as a container for introductory content or a set of navigation links at the top of a page?
A)<nav>
B)<head>
C)<header> // answer
D)<top></top>

✅ Correct Answer: C. <header>

Easy English Explanation

The <header> element is used for the top part of a webpage or a section.

It usually contains:

📝 Title (Heading)
📄 Logo
🔗 Navigation links (<nav>)
Example:
<header>
  <h1>My Website</h1>
  <nav>
    <a href="#">Home</a>
    <a href="#">About</a>
  </nav>
</header>
Why the other options are wrong?
❌ A. <nav> → Only for navigation links, not the whole top section.
❌ B. <head> → Stores page information (title, CSS, metadata). It is not visible on the webpage.
❌ D. <top> → This is not a valid HTML tag.
Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
<header> = Head of the page 👤
<nav> = Navigation (menu) 🧭
<head> = Hidden information ⚙️
One-Line Memory:

<header> = Top part of a page with logo, title, or navigation.

✅ Answer: C. <header></header>


7.Why was accessibility difficult in HTML4-based layouts?
A)There were no links
B)Screen readers couldn't distinguish between a header, a footer, or an ad  //answers
C)Images were not allowed
D)Text was always small


✅ Correct Answer: B. Screen readers couldn't distinguish between a header, a footer, or an ad.

Easy English Explanation

In HTML4, developers mostly used only <div> tags.

Example:

<div>Header</div>
<div>Advertisement</div>
<div>Main Content</div>
<div>Footer</div>

For a screen reader (used by visually impaired people), every section looks like just a <div>.

So it cannot easily tell:

Which is the header
Which is the main content
Which is the footer
Which is an advertisement

This made accessibility difficult.

HTML5 Solution

HTML5 introduced semantic tags:

<header>...</header>
<main>...</main>
<aside>Advertisement</aside>
<footer>...</footer>

Now screen readers can understand each part of the page much better.

Easy Trick to Remember 🧠
HTML4 = Only <div> = Screen reader gets confused 😕
HTML5 = Semantic tags = Screen reader understands 👍
One-Line Memory:

HTML4 used only <div>, so screen readers couldn't identify different page sections.

✅ Answer: B. Screen readers couldn't distinguish between a header, a footer, or an ad.


